---
import Section from './Section.astro';

// What the market funds, and where the engine sits under it. The directions are
// listed above the smallest systems, which is the ground the engine holds.
const directions = [
  {
    direction: 'Quality intelligence',
    supplies: 'the fault question answered on the line: which readings moved together in the seconds before it',
  },
  {
    direction: 'Adaptive operations and supply',
    supplies: "the system's own recent history, queried with no round trip",
  },
  {
    direction: 'Operations data backbone',
    supplies: 'the source record below MES, PLC, ERP and LIMS, held on the system',
  },
  {
    direction: 'Autonomous and flexible operations',
    supplies: 'an accountable record on the system itself, with no server in the loop',
  },
  {
    direction: 'Product and supply provenance',
    supplies: 'several axes in one address, checked by recomputation',
  },
];

const markets = [
  {
    market: 'Embedded database systems',
    figure: 'US$ 11.71B in 2025 to US$ 23.55B in 2034, 8.08% a year',
  },
  { market: 'Physical AI', figure: 'US$ 0.89B in 2025 to US$ 15.28B in 2032, 47.2% a year' },
];
---

<Section
  id="market"
  label="Market"
  title="The buyer carries the system long after it ships"
  lede="The buyer is the organization that builds or operates an autonomous system and carries responsibility for it long after it ships. A product company licenses the engine and ships it inside its own system. A technology partner licenses it once and uses it across the systems it delivers to several clients. An operator or systems integrator licenses Delta to command a mixed fleet."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      That buyer sits in every field the product serves: a defense program, a medical device
      maker, a vehicle program, and an energy operator. Each carries a different duty, and the
      requirement is one: the system has to hold its own record and answer from it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Direction the market funds</th>
          <th>What the engine supplies beneath it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          directions.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="lead">{row.direction}</td>
              <td>{row.supplies}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="stack">
    <h3>Size of the field</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Market</th>
            <th>Published estimate</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            markets.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="lead">{row.market}</td>
                <td>{row.figure}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Both figures are third-party published estimates, for the embedded database system market
      and for physical AI.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="stack">
    <h3>The wedge</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Roughly 10,000 firmware teams worldwide, in industrial equipment, instruments, and
        modules, where the memory budget is the purchase requirement. The count is derived from
        published figures on industrial automation equipment makers, IoT device manufacturers, and
        embedded system companies, narrowed to those that ship a microcontroller product with a
        multi-axis history requirement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Moving the record off the system costs the buyer cloud egress for the life of the product,
        answers that stop when the link is down, and the engineering months a team spends building
        a multi-axis store in-house. Europe is a natural first market, where the German-speaking
        industrial base is the densest concentration of the buyer profile in the wedge.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .stack > * + * {
    margin-top: 18px;
  }
</style>
